    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        Agility Bandits was created as a responsive blog for a fictional dog-agility training
        centre. Visitors needed to be able to browse and read articles without an account. An
        authenticated administrator also needed access to the tools for creating, editing, and
        deleting posts. Since the finished website would load its content from an API, the layouts
        had to work with different images, titles, excerpts, and article lengths.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            Dog agility is fast, energetic, and playful, and I wanted the website to reflect that.
            At the same time, it was still a blog where people might spend time reading longer
            articles. The main challenge was creating a strong visual identity without making the
            content difficult to scan or tiring to read.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Give the brand an energetic personality while keeping articles readable
            </li>
            <li>
              Create flexible layouts that could handle content coming from an API
            </li>
            <li>
              Keep the public reading experience separate from the admin tools
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--explore'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/design-agilitybanditsmoodboard-600.webp'
            data-src='design-agilitybanditsmoodboard-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='517'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Agility Bandits mood board showing energetic colours, typography, dogs, and sport-inspired graphics'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Mood board exploring movement, energy, and a friendly visual style
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              I used strong colours and playful graphic details to communicate the energy of the
              sport. The article layouts are simpler so the text and images remain the main focus
              while reading.
            </li>
            <li>
              I designed the cards to work with different title and excerpt lengths instead of
              relying on every article having the same amount of content.
            </li>
            <li>
              The public journey moves from discovering an article to opening and reading it. The
              admin flow has its own clear actions for signing in and managing posts.
            </li>
            <li>
              I planned hover, focus, validation, and navigation states in the design so users
              receive clear feedback when interacting with the interface.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--booking'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/design-agilitybandits-600.webp'
            data-src='design-agilitybandits-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='495'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Agility Bandits desktop wireframes and high-fidelity blog layouts in Figma'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Wireframes and finished layouts for browsing, reading, and managing articles
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The final design combines a playful identity with a practical blog structure that can adapt
        to real API content. Working on both the public pages and the admin flow helped me think
        about the website from two different perspectives. Readers need a simple way to find and
        enjoy the content, while the administrator needs clear controls for keeping that content up
        to date. It also taught me to test layouts with less predictable content instead of
        designing every card around an ideal title or image.
      </p>
    </div>

